{"event": "command_result", "time": 1793338207.1733985, "run_id": "6be06d8a12304096898a8a4f0af98725", "seq": 517, "argv": ["/mnt/d/Muiti-Agent Programing/experiments/code-agent-smolagents/24371220/.venv/bin/python", "read_pdf.py", "--start-page", "23", "--end-page", "23"], "cwd": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/stability-paper-10-gkvnan7w/results/paper/repo", "rc": 0, "stdout": "{\"pdf\": \"/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/stability-paper-10-gkvnan7w/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf\", \"pages\": [{\"page\": 23, \"text\": \"                                                                                                                  Agentic LLM     •   1:23\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                  Fig. 13. Multi LLM agent framework with a planner, caller, summarizer [Shen et al., 2024]\\n\\n\\n\\nvision language model, they achieve target object grounding, achieving improved performance on an L-ZSON\\nbenchmark.\\n3.2.2 Action Tools. As we have seen in Section 2.3, LLM results may be augmented with results that are retrieved\\nfrom external sources, such as search engines. The ability to call search engines can be generalized to calling\\nother tools. When the application programming interface (API) of these tools is known, LLMs can be integrated\\neasily with them. To an LLM, an API is just another language to learn. Agentic LLMs must be trained to decide\\nwhen and how to utilize external tools, depending on the task [Shen et al., 2024]. Language models can teach\\nthemselves to use tools [Schick et al., 2023]. The Toolformer model is trained to decide which APIs to call, when\\nto call them, what arguments to pass, and how to best incorporate the results into future token prediction. A\\nrange of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a\\ncalendar. Further works extend this to a larger range of tools. ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs\\nfrom RapidAPI, a large dataset of publicly available REST APIs.4\\n   Another framework is EasyTool [Yuan et al., 2024c], which focuses on structured and unified instructions\\nfrom tool documentations, building on ToolBench. ToolAlpaca is a benchmark with over 3938 instances from 400\\nAPIs [Tang et al., 2023]. A tool-based benchmark for question answering is ToolQA [Zhuang et al., 2023]. Gorilla\\nis a finetuned LLaMa-based model for generating API calls [Patil et al., 2023], also introducing the APIBench\\nbenchmark. Many tool calling frameworks have been developed.\\n   Selecting the right tool and summarizing its result are difficult skills. Zhao et al. [2024a] study how LLMs\\ncan improve recommendation through tool learning. Another approach also suggests to use an LLM for this\\ntask [Shen et al., 2024]. They use different LLMs for (1) reasoning ability, (2) request writing, and (3) result\\nsummarization. Figure 13 illustrates this architecture, consisting of a planner, a caller, and a summarizer, each\\nimplemented by a different LLM finetuned for its specific capability. Good results are reported on the LLMs\\n\\n4 https://rapidapi.com/hub\\n\\n\\n\\n                                          Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}]}\n", "stderr": "", "timed_out": false}
{"event": "tool_result", "time": 1793338207.1852942, "run_id": "6be06d8a12304096898a8a4f0af98725", "seq": 518, "action": {"tool": "run_command", "argv": ["python", "read_pdf.py", "--start-page", "23", "--end-page", "23"], "cwd": "."}, "result": {"argv": ["/mnt/d/Muiti-Agent Programing/experiments/code-agent-smolagents/24371220/.venv/bin/python", "read_pdf.py", "--start-page", "23", "--end-page", "23"], "cwd": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/stability-paper-10-gkvnan7w/results/paper/repo", "rc": 0, "stdout": "{\"pdf\": \"/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/stability-paper-10-gkvnan7w/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf\", \"pages\": [{\"page\": 23, \"text\": \"                                                                                                                  Agentic LLM     •   1:23\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                  Fig. 13. Multi LLM agent framework with a planner, caller, summarizer [Shen et al., 2024]\\n\\n\\n\\nvision language model, they achieve target object grounding, achieving improved performance on an L-ZSON\\nbenchmark.\\n3.2.2 Action Tools. As we have seen in Section 2.3, LLM results may be augmented with results that are retrieved\\nfrom external sources, such as search engines. The ability to call search engines can be generalized to calling\\nother tools. When the application programming interface (API) of these tools is known, LLMs can be integrated\\neasily with them. To an LLM, an API is just another language to learn. Agentic LLMs must be trained to decide\\nwhen and how to utilize external tools, depending on the task [Shen et al., 2024]. Language models can teach\\nthemselves to use tools [Schick et al., 2023]. The Toolformer model is trained to decide which APIs to call, when\\nto call them, what arguments to pass, and how to best incorporate the results into future token prediction. A\\nrange of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a\\ncalendar. Further works extend this to a larger range of tools. ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs\\nfrom RapidAPI, a large dataset of publicly available REST APIs.4\\n   Another framework is EasyTool [Yuan et al., 2024c], which focuses on structured and unified instructions\\nfrom tool documentations, building on ToolBench. ToolAlpaca is a benchmark with over 3938 instances from 400\\nAPIs [Tang et al., 2023]. A tool-based benchmark for question answering is ToolQA [Zhuang et al., 2023]. Gorilla\\nis a finetuned LLaMa-based model for generating API calls [Patil et al., 2023], also introducing the APIBench\\nbenchmark. Many tool calling frameworks have been developed.\\n   Selecting the right tool and summarizing its result are difficult skills. Zhao et al. [2024a] study how LLMs\\ncan improve recommendation through tool learning. Another approach also suggests to use an LLM for this\\ntask [Shen et al., 2024]. They use different LLMs for (1) reasoning ability, (2) request writing, and (3) result\\nsummarization. Figure 13 illustrates this architecture, consisting of a planner, a caller, and a summarizer, each\\nimplemented by a different LLM finetuned for its specific capability. Good results are reported on the LLMs\\n\\n4 https://rapidapi.com/hub\\n\\n\\n\\n                                          Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}]}\n", "stderr": "", "timed_out": false, "evidence_ids": ["ev-731fa63b8d34dde45e62"], "delivery": "Originals queued for the next model request; large batches may span requests."}}
